WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.300
As a film maker, Christopher Nolan always wants to walk a fine line.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.200
If there's one fundamental theme that suffuses his entire filmography,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.080
it's that cinema, as a shared narrative, can be a hugely powerful cultural force.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.300
I'm far from the first to notice or mention that many of his films reference film itself

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.140
that, for example, the Inception team bears a strong resemblance to a film crew.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:53.400
But interestingly, though a lot of his work could be called metacinematic.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:59.040
Nolan is extremely careful about avoiding metacinematic images in his work.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.340
For example, in the established Batman continuity, Bruce Wayne and his parents

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:12.000
are out to see The Mask of Zorro film before the fateful double murder that incites the Dark Knight's whole saga.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.080
In Batman Begins, however, Nolan's retelling of that origin, the Waynes are out to the opera Mefistofele instead.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.500
That change is a purposeful one, as Nolan has said in an interview,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.440
"We didn't have young Bruce Wayne going to see Zorro because a character in a movie watching a movie

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.120
is very different than a character in a comic book watching a movie...

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.820
It creats a deconstructionist thing that we were trying to avoid."

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:46.220
The reason Nolan tries to avoid this is because, over and above everything, his cinema has always been about immersion,

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:53.400
bringing you into a story so fully that the edges disappear and you're carried along by the narrative momentum.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:00.660
This is the line that Nolan want's to walk. He wants to be immersive and metacinematic at the same time.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.800
In other words, he wants to hide in plain sight.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:12.220
Hiding in plain sight is essentially the subject matter of Nolan's fifth film and my favorite, The Prestige.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:18.180
You can almost call this film meta-meta-cinematic in that it functions as a kind of rule book

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:24.200
for how to achieve this immersive effect without seeming deconstructionist, to use Nolan's words.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.200
Just take the first sequence, the first thing you see is the film title

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.420
over a mysterious shot of several mislaid top hats in the woods.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:40.720
The text here is, of course, the title card, but if you know the film, you know that this text also has a literal function

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.880
because these hats are the prestige of The Prestige itself.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.540
The second clue is in their number, doubles, copies, multiplicity,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.960
is the key to understanding the tricks of this film and this is echoed in the second shot as well,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.300
with bird cages full of identical canaries.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.820
From here, Michael Caine's character, goes on to describe how a magic trick works.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:13.080
"Every magic trick consists of three parts or acts," the pledge, the turn, and finally, the prestige.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.400
The sequence carefully sets up the film's own pledges, that is

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.040
Borden and Angier played by Christian Bale and Hugh Jackman respectively,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.620
while illustrating its method through Michael Caine's voice-over and his trick for the little girl.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.860
But the film is playing it's own tricks here through editing.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.540
Where is this voice-over coming from?

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:38.580
Usually in film, a voice-over means someone commenting on the events being shown from sometime in the future.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.100
When the scene ends, it cuts to the court room scene of Borden's murder trial, picking up

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.960
the last phrase as if Michael Caine's whole explanation was testimony.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.740
It's not until the very end of the film that we learn that the bird trick with the girl

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.700
is chronologically the last moment in the film.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:01.000
Nolan reverses the temporal relationship between the voice-over and the scene under it.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.320
And this kind of displacement is the key mechanism of the whole movie.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:12.180
The Prestige is all about a trick that moves its object through time and space instantaneously.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.880
This is exactly what all film editing does.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.600
Most editing cuts between short distances and continuous times indeed with a simple cut,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.320
that's what most people expect.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:30.280
But that same simple cut can traverse great distances also and great length of time in either direction.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.760
Nolan has described learning the power of this by watching Terrence Malick's  The Thin Red Line

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.540
in which Malick cuts the memories simply without blurs or fades or wavy lines

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.460
and the powerful effect this can have on a viewer.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.920
The Prestige exploits this power to the extreme, cutting between multiple different nested memories

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.040
Nolan sets up this device with Borden and Angier's respective journals

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.760
once these devices are established, he cuts between the narratives at will and without warning.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.940
In this way, The Prestige demonstrates a unique capacity of film

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.180
without compromising the audiences' suspended  disbelief.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:13.160
The complex narrative structure is totally subservient to the story Nolan wants to tell.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.420
It's necessary to keep it's twist secret until the film wants to reveal them.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:24.460
This is Christopher Nolan's great gift as a filmmaker, he's so in tune with the dynamics of film narrative

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.600
that he can construct a plot with so much forward momentum

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:35.280
that even when he gives you all the clues, you remain at his mercy until the very final shot.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.560
Now if this was the only lesson of The Prestige I'd be satisfied and calling it a great film

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.720
But there's one scene here, easily missed, that adds a final, important point, I think.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.400
-He killed it.    -What?

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.380
No, he killed it.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.940
See? It's alright, it's fine.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:03.100
But where's it's brother?

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.280
You can watched The Prestige and enjoy the stoy for what it is,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.420
that's what Nolan wants for us, he wants us to be amazed,

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:15.700
and as the film itself says, most of us want to be fooled.

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:22.840
But all films, even one as tightly whelmed as this, invite a probing eye.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:29.120
As the boy sees into the bird trick, we can see into cinema.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.940
We're accustomed to taking most editing for granted, but the way stories are told,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:40.760
the tools of any story-telling medium in large part determine the way we construct our own memories,

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.680
shared or personal.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:48.240
This is all to say, that studying film doesn't kill it's magic,

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.800
it feeds that magic back into the real world.

